## What it means

Only one program runs per input, so most of their values never meet. The packed residual stream is about as wide as the widest program, not the sum of all of them. Because every weight matrix spans the residual width, block-diagonal stacking costs width x columns, which grows like K^2, while packing keeps the width nearly fixed and grows close to K. One dense model still holds every program's heads and units at the full shared width, so it costs more than the separate models it replaces; its gain is over stacking them into one model.

## What it does not prove

- Only the full 10-program pack is checked per config; the smaller packs of the K sweep are built by the same code and only measured. Unit tests check 2-10 program packs on every input up to length 4.
- Sampled configs are checked on a few hundred inputs, not all of them.
- The clique bound and the integer program are optimal for this conflict rule only: no erasing, all programs starting at layer 0, one dim per value. Other rules can give narrower models.
- The K sweep uses one program order; other orders give other curves.
- Gates are very large weights (see max gate). They are exact here, but such a model is not a sensible starting point for training.
- Dense params include zeros inside kept blocks.

